NAME
    screen - displays, seats, and screen resolution

SYNOPSIS
    screen [list]
    screen next | <index>
    screen res [auto | max | <W>x<H>]

DESCRIPTION
    screen manages display seats (each GPU+screen pair is an independent shell)
    and the screen resolution policy.

    Seats:
      (none)/list   List displays with resolution, depth, and the active one.
      next          Switch focus to the next display.
      <index>       Switch focus to display <index>.

RESOLUTION
    TOS does not force the GPU to its maximum resolution. On a tier-3 GPU + a
    large screen the maximum is 160x50, which renders the TUI in tiny text. We
    cannot scale glyphs, but we can lower the resolution: fewer character cells
    over the same physical blocks means bigger, readable text.

    'screen res' with no value shows the current resolution, the hardware
    maximum, the physical screen size in blocks, and the active policy.

    'screen res <value>' (ADMIN) sets the policy, applies it immediately, and
    saves it to /etc/tos.cfg (screenRes) for the next boot:

      auto    Density-based (the default): target ~10 columns x 4 rows per
              physical screen block, clamped to the hardware max, so text stays
              a readable size on any screen. Falls back to ~80x25 when the
              block size can't be read. Tune with screenColsPerBlock /
              screenRowsPerBlock in /etc/tos.cfg.
      max     Use the hardware maximum (the old behavior).
      WxH     An explicit resolution, e.g. 80x25. Clamped to the max, with a
              warning if the request didn't fit.

PROGRAMS
    A packaged program can declare the resolution it needs in its manifest:

        screen = { width = 60, height = 20, mode = "exact" }   -- or "min"

    TOS fits the display before running it (warning if the screen's max is too
    small) and restores the boot policy when it exits. "min" leaves the screen
    alone if it is already at least that large.

EXAMPLES
    screen res
    screen res 80x25
    screen res auto

SEE ALSO
    man boot, doctor, MANUAL.md (Chapter 10)
